{
    "title": "Delegation of Authority Under Section 208(d)(6) of the Compact of Free Association Amendments Act of 2024",
    "type": "Presidential Document",
    "date": "December 19, 2025",
    "url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2025-12-19/pdf/2025-23568.pdf",
    "summary": "The President has given the Secretary of State the job of sending a required report to Congress about the Compact of Free Association Amendments Act of 2024. This is an administrative step that shifts who formally delivers the report, without changing the law or its goals.\n\nThis move likely helps the State Department and Congress by streamlining communication. The department already manages many parts of the relationships covered by the Compact, so letting it prepare and submit the report can make the process faster and more consistent. That can benefit lawmakers who need timely updates, federal agencies that coordinate on these policies, and communities and partners connected to the Compact who rely on clear information and steady oversight.\n\nThere may be some downsides. Some people may prefer these reports to come directly from the White House to signal higher-level attention. Others might worry about accountability if responsibilities are spread out, or about delays if the department faces competing priorities. For most people, though, this change won\u2019t be felt day to day. It is about how the government organizes its work, not about changing commitments, funding, or programs under the Compact.\n\nOverall, this is a routine step to keep government reporting running smoothly: the State Department will submit the required report to Congress, and the memorandum making that change is being published for public record.",
